**Interface Segregation Principle (ISP).** Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use; prefer several small, focused interfaces over one large one.

Violation: a single `Machine` interface with `print()`, `scan()`, `fax()`. A basic printer must implement `scan()` and `fax()` and throw `UnsupportedOperationException`. Fix:

    interface Printer { void print(Doc d); }
    interface Scanner { void scan(Doc d); }
    interface Fax     { void fax(Doc d); }

    class BasicPrinter implements Printer { ... }
    class OfficeMachine implements Printer, Scanner, Fax { ... }

Benefits: no empty or throwing methods, smaller change surface, easier mocking. A payments example: split `PaymentProcessor` into `Authorizer`, `Refunder` and `Reporter`, so a read-only reporting client cannot call refund.

**Template Method pattern.** A base class defines the skeleton of an algorithm in a final method and defers some steps to subclasses.

    abstract class ReportJob {
        public final void run() { load(); validate(); process(); save(); }
        protected abstract void process();       // varies
        protected void validate() { }            // optional hook
    }

The fixed order lives in one place; subclasses override only the varying steps. Use it when several classes share the same workflow and differ in details (import jobs, data pipelines). Trade-off: inheritance couples subclasses to the base class; strategy (composition) is the alternative when behaviour should be swapped at runtime.

**Code reviews.** A good answer describes a process:
- Check correctness and edge cases first, then tests, readability, naming, design and security/performance concerns.
- Keep reviews small, run the build and tests, and automate style with linters so humans focus on logic.
- Give specific, kind, actionable comments; ask questions rather than dictating; separate must-fix from nits.
- Review promptly, and accept feedback on your own code gracefully.
